= From the desk of Localization

The QA team asked for a one-page smoke test that proves each of
our supported locales survives the typesetting pipeline with
diacritics intact. We declined to write the poetry ourselves.
The national pangrams already do it better, and they are, for
tax-residency reasons, the property of no one.

**Polski.** *Zażółć gęślą jaźń.* Łódź leży nad Wisłą, a Kraków
nie. Pchnąć w tę łódź jeża lub ośm skrzyń fig. The second
sentence is, we are reliably informed, a tongue-twister. We
have not verified it ourselves and we are not going to.

**Čeština.** *Příliš žluťoučký kůň úpěl ďábelské ódy.* Vrh
syn trh. The horse, we gather, was too yellow. The reviewer
who suggested this pangram did not elaborate.

**Magyar.** *Árvíztűrő tükörfúrógép.* Egy hűtlen vejét
fülöncsípő, dühös mexikói úr. The first phrase translates,
literally, to "flood-resistant mirror-drilling machine." We
have decided not to ask. The second phrase introduces an
angry Mexican gentleman pinching his unfaithful son-in-law's
ear, which seems, all things considered, like a reasonable
response.

**Română.** *Bună ziua! Mâine plec la mare cu fătul meu.* QA
flagged this one specifically. Romanian's comma-below
diacritics (ț, ș) have historically rendered as Turkish-style
cedillas (ţ, ş) when the reader's font substitutes Helvetica
and ships only the cedilla form. The embedded Noto Sans path
sidesteps that entirely: the comma-below glyphs come from the
font we bundled, not from whatever Helvetica clone the reader
fell back to.

**Matematyka.** 2 + 3 ≤ 6, √16 = 4, ∂f/∂x ≠ 0, ∑(1..n). The
math operators are not part of the Noto Sans Regular cut. They
are not part of the **Italic** or **Bold** cuts either. They
route — automatically, mid-line, with no `#set text(font:
...)` ceremony — through the bundled Noto Sans Math cut, which
the layout engine selects per-cluster whenever the primary
face returns `.notdef`. Latin → Math → Latin happens inside a
single visual word without you having to think about it.

If any line above looks wrong to you, copy it out of the PDF
and paste it into a text editor. The /ToUnicode CMap is wired
through every embedded face, so you will get back exactly the
codepoints that went in — diacritics, comma-below, math
operators, all of it. If your text editor cannot display them
afterwards, that is a conversation between you and your text
editor. We are not part of it.

Signed, the Localization Team. Please do not call us. We are
in a meeting until further notice.
